Time and date, on row 2 alongside position.

** Not `display-time'
The manual is explicit that `display-time' works by setting
`global-mode-string' to refer to `display-time-string'. Emacs' default
`mode-line-format' uses that variable, so this is the sanctioned way to show
a clock — and it would work here too, except that the bar composes its own
`mode-line-format' and does not include `global-mode-string'. Adding that
variable back just to get a clock would reintroduce a construct this tree
deliberately does not publish, and would put the time in the default position
rather than on row 2.

So the clock is a segment like any other: its own cached string, its own
accent, its own place in the order file.

** Segment
#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(defvar manifolding-modeline--clock-cache nil
  "Cached \"HH:MM day date\" string. Rebuilt on the bar's cache tick.")

(defun manifolding-modeline--clock-refresh ()
  "Recompute the clock cache. One `format-time-string', once per tick."
  (setq manifolding-modeline--clock-cache
        (format-time-string "%H:%M %a %d %b")))

(manifolding-modeline-define-segment clock
  :doc "Time and date, cached on the bar's tick."
  :face nil
  :side :right
  :construct 'manifolding-modeline--clock-cache
  :refresh '(manifolding-modeline--clock-refresh))
#+end_src

** Layout
Twenty-four-hour time, then weekday, day and month. The date is short on
purpose: this sits on a ~40-column bar opposite the position, and
"Sat 26 Sep" costs less than it is worth on a phone. `manifolding-modeline-
clock-interval' (30s) is the refresh period, so the minute is never more
than half a minute stale — and because the bar redraws on every keystroke
anyway, a sharper clock would cost redraws for no visible benefit.
